f I can form a chow early, that's good. So keeping W3 is important. Therefore, the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. So between T5, J1, J3, T9, B6, B9, T2.\n\nT2 is part of a possible chow if I get T1 and T3. But I don't have those. So T2 is a single tile. Similarly for the others.\n\nPerhaps the best option is to discard T5. Because T5 is a middle tile that might be part of a chow, but since I don't have the surrounding tiles, it's not helpful. Discarding it would free up a tile to work on the W1, W2, W3 chow.\n\nSo the answer would be to play T5.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential for forming a W1-W2-W3 chow. Discarding T5, which is a middle tile without supporting chow possibilities, allows focusing on developing the character suit melds. Keeping W1, W2, and W3 intact preserves the opportunity to complete this chow, while T5 has limited utility in the current hand structure.\n\nReason: T5 is a single tile with no adjacent tiles for chow formation. Discarding it prioritizes the development of the W1-W2-W3 chow, which is more impactful for building a winning hand. Other options like J1/J3 risk giving honors to opponents, while keeping W3 is critical for the potential chow.\n\nAnswer: Play T5"}
