for a chow. So maybe not.\\n\\nIf I discard W5, the hand would have W1, W3, W6, W7 (2), T7, T9, B3, B6, F4. Again, not sure how to form melds. Maybe there's a way to make a chow with W3, W5, W7? But I don't have a W5 anymore. So no.\\n\\nDiscarding W3 would leave me with W1, W5, W6, W7 (2), T7, T9, B3, B6, F4. Still not obvious. Maybe W5, W6, W7 is a chow? I have one W5, one W6, and two W7. So that's two W7s. Not enough for a chow. So no.\\n\\nDiscarding W7 would take one of the two W7s. Then I have W1, W3, W5, W6, W7 (1), T7, T9, B3, B6, F4. Maybe the remaining W7 can form a pair. But I don't see how to make four melds and a pair. The existing pung of W2. Then maybe W5, W6, W7 is a chow? If I have one W5, one W6, and one W7, that's a chow. So if I discard one W7, I have one W7 left. Then the chow would be W5, W6, W7. Then the pair would be W7? Wait, no, the pair would be the remaining W7. Wait, but I have two W7s. If I discard one, I have one left. So the pair would be W7? No, because there's only one. Hmm. Maybe not. Maybe I need to discard a different tile.\\n\\nDiscarding T7: Then my hand would be W1, W3, W5, W6, W7 (2), T9, B3, B6, F4. The T7 is gone. Maybe I can form a chow with W5, W6, W7. That's a chow. Then the pung of W2. Then the remaining tiles would be W1, W3, T9, B3, B6, F4. That's not enough. So maybe not.\\n\\nDiscarding T9: Then the hand has W1, W3, W5, W6, W7 (2), T7, B3, B6, F4. The T9 is gone. Again, not sure.\\n\\nDiscarding B3: Then the hand has W1, W3, W5, W6, W7 (2), T7, T9, B6, F4. Maybe B6 can pair with another B6? No, I have one B6. So not helpful.\\n\\nDiscarding B6: Similar to above. Not helpful.\\n\\nDiscarding F4: Then the hand has W1, W3, W5, W6, W7 (2), T7, T9, B3, B6. Maybe the F4 is part of a pair? Not sure.\\n\\nWait, maybe I should look for possible winning patterns. Let's see. The meld is a pung of W2. Let's see if the remaining tiles can form four melds and a pair. Let's list the remaining tiles:\\n\\nW1, W3, W5, W6, W7 (2), T7