they can get B4 or B5 for a chow with B3, or B8 for a chow with B6 and B7. \n\nSince the user has to discard a tile, maybe they should discard a tile that's not part of any possible future meld. Looking at the options, maybe T9 is the least useful for the user's own hand. They have a T9 in their hand, but no other T tiles except T9. Unless they can form a T9 pung, but they only have one. So discarding T9 might be safe. \n\nAlternatively, if they discard J2, the next player can kong, which is bad. So better to avoid that. So the best options are to discard T9, W3, B3, or maybe W6. \n\nWait, the user's hand has W3, W6, W7. If they discard W6, someone might take it for a chow. The player across has a B4-B5-B6 chow. If they discard B6, the player across might take it to complete B4-B5-B6. But the player across already has that chow. So maybe discarding B6 is safe. \n\nHmm. This is tricky. Maybe the best move is to discard T9, as it's a terminal tile and the user doesn't have others to form a meld with it. Also, the player across already has a T9 in their chow, so discarding it might not help them. So the safest tile to discard is T9.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has a kong of W2 and a chow of W7-W8-W9. The T9 is a terminal tile not needed for further melds, and the player across already has a T9 in their chow. Discarding T9 avoids giving others a chance to complete a chow or kong.\nReason: T9 is a terminal tile not essential for the user's hand and the player across already has a T9 chow, minimizing risk. Discarding J2 could allow the next player to kong, which is riskier.\nAnswer: Play T9"}
